---
// Bridge set, Phase 4 to 5 (CURRICULUM_GAPS_PLAN.md Section 6, item 3). Data: src/data/sets/bridge-4-5.yaml. Not a topic.
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import SetTable, { type SetRow } from '../components/SetTable';
import { loadData } from '../lib/data';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const data = await loadData();
const set = data.sets['bridge-4-5'];
const num = (d: string) => (/^\d+$/.test(d) ? Number(d) : 0);
const rows: SetRow[] = [...set.problems]
  .sort((a, b) => num(a.difficulty) - num(b.difficulty) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id))
  .map((p) => ({ id: p.id, title: p.title, url: p.url, source: p.source, difficulty: p.difficulty, summary: p.summary, have: p.have, newIdea: p.new_idea }));
---
<StarlightPage frontmatter={{ title: 'Bridge set: Phase 4 to 5', description: 'Problems rated about 1900 to 2100 that need one step beyond the tools of Phase 4.' }}>
  <p>
    Phase 4 ends around rating 2000 and Phase 5 starts at 1800, but the problems of the two bands feel different: a Phase 4 problem is one graph tool applied well,
    and a problem of 1900 to 2100 is usually a graph tool plus one extra step. This set has {set.problems.length} such problems.
    For each one you see <strong>what you already have</strong> (a tool from Phase 4 or earlier) and, hidden behind a button, <strong>the one new idea</strong> that completes the solution.
    Every statement was opened and read, and every idea was checked against it. No problem here needs a topic from Phase 5.
  </p>
  <h2 id="how-to-use">How to use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Take it after the Phase 4 exam</strong> and before or alongside Phase 5, as the next step up from the Phase 4 ladders.</li>
    <li><strong>Work the way the <a href={url('/method/')}>Method</a> says:</strong> write the budget and the candidate tools first. Then read the "what you have" column only if you are stuck, and open the new idea last: the struggle is the practice.</li>
    <li><strong>After the idea, ask where else it applies.</strong> Each new idea is a small tool of its own (a pigeonhole bound, a long-path decomposition, merging small into large). Write the one you met in the log note so you can recognise it later.</li>
    <li>Difficulty is the rating on the platform (here AtCoder Problems difficulty), so a number on this page is not comparable to a Codeforces rating one-to-one.</li>
  </ul>
  <SetTable client:load rows={rows} kind="bridge" />
</StarlightPage>
